Output lost per quarter of delay, at the reference gain of 0.30. Each series is one institution, plotted against the reporting lag $\ell$ with the effective delay $k=\ell+1$ shown on the axis because the theory's $g^\star(k)$ refers to $k$. The institutions that must compute from current data to act at all — the central linear programme, material balance and priority rationing — lose an order of magnitude more output per period of delay than the two whose plans are built from stocks and prices. Tâtonnement is flat to slightly rising, which is what a mechanism that does not chase a stale signal should do. Generated by `paper/make_figures.py` from `paper/tables/main_lag_grid.csv`.
